<h1>Filtering Matrix Dimensions</h1>
<p>A <strong>dimension filter</strong> changes which values a dimension offers, so the matrix produces a different
	set of cases. It follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/">Matrix and Cases</a>, which introduces the matrix and
	the cases generated from it, and it works on the same test.</p>
<p>This is not the same thing as clearing a check mark in the <strong>Select</strong> column. Case selection leaves
	the matrix alone and unticks combinations that exist; a filter takes a value away, the matrix is rebuilt without
	it, and the cases carrying that value are never generated at all. The two are told apart by what the panel
	reports, and the difference is visible in a single number.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Matrix and Cases</strong> test. Its four dimensions produce
	<code>3 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 24</code> cases, all of them selected:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/cases-before-filtering.png", "Matrix and Cases run panel before filtering, reporting Total Cases 24 and a Test Cases group header reading 24 of 24", "large") }}
<p>Two readings matter here. <strong>Total Cases: 24</strong> is what the run will execute, and the
	<strong>Test Cases (24/24)</strong> group header is selected cases over generated cases. The second number is
	the size of the matrix itself, and it is the one a filter moves.</p>

<h2 id="opening-the-filter">Opening the filter</h2>
<p>Click a dimension's column header in the case table — <strong>algorithm</strong>, <strong>seed</strong>,
	<strong>height</strong> or <strong>width</strong>. Each header opens the filter for that dimension only:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/dimension-filter-dialog.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the algorithm dimension with ASTAR, BFS and DFS all checked", "large") }}
<p>The dialog is titled <strong>Dimension Filter: algorithm</strong> and lists every value the dimension offers,
	each one checked. A checked value stays in the matrix; an unchecked value is removed from it.
	<strong>Select All</strong> and <strong>Clear All</strong> apply to the whole list.</p>

<h3 id="adding-a-value">Adding a value the matrix never declared</h3>
<p>The <strong>Add value</strong> field works in the other direction. Type a value, click <strong>Add</strong>, and
	it joins the list already checked, marked with <code>✶</code> to distinguish it from the values the test
	declared. The entry is read as an integer when it looks like one, otherwise as a floating-point number,
	otherwise as text — so <code>7</code> arrives as a number and <code>GREEDY</code> as a string. Adding a value
	that is already listed simply checks it.</p>
<p>A dimension can therefore be widened as well as narrowed from the panel, without editing the test source. The
	field is present only while <strong>Random Configuration</strong> is set to <strong>Disabled</strong>; a
	dimension that draws its values randomly is covered on its own page.</p>

<h2 id="keeping-one-value">Keeping a single value</h2>
<p>Clear <strong>ASTAR</strong> and <strong>DFS</strong>, leaving <strong>BFS</strong> checked, then click
	<strong class="text-accent">Apply</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/keeping-a-single-value.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the algorithm dimension with only BFS checked and ASTAR and DFS cleared", "large") }}
<p><strong>Apply</strong> commits the dialog's state to the panel and leaves the dialog open, so several
	adjustments can be judged one after another. <strong>OK</strong> applies and closes,
	<strong>Cancel</strong> discards anything not yet applied, and <strong>Reset to Defaults</strong> restores this
	one dimension to the values the test declared.</p>
<p>Close the dialog. The panel has been rebuilt around the surviving value:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/cases-after-filtering.png", "Matrix and Cases run panel after filtering algorithm to BFS, reporting Total Cases 8, a Test Cases group header reading 8 of 8, and the algorithm column header marked as changed", "large") }}
<p>The table now holds eight rows, all of them BFS, renumbered <strong>Case</strong> 1 to 8. The
	<strong>algorithm</strong> column header is marked as changed, and the <code>🔸</code> badge reports that the
	panel no longer matches the committed plan.</p>
<p>The group header is the telling one: it reads <strong>Test Cases (8/8)</strong>, not <code>8/24</code>. Eight
	cases are selected out of eight that exist. Had the same eight been reached by clearing check marks in the
	<strong>Select</strong> column, the header would have read <code>8/24</code> — the sixteen ASTAR and DFS cases
	would still be there, merely unticked. They are gone because the values that produced them are gone.</p>

<h2 id="the-plan-holds">The plan holds until it is committed</h2>
<p>The panel is a proposal. Look at the test's row in the execution tree while the filter sits on the panel
	uncommitted:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/plan-before-committing-the-filter.png", "Execution tree with the Matrix and Cases row still reporting Plan 24 while the panel shows eight cases", "large") }}
<p><strong>Plan</strong> still counts <strong>24</strong>. The tree reports what was committed, not what is
	currently on the panel, so a filter can be tried, judged and abandoned without disturbing the recorded plan or
	the runs measured against it.</p>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> and confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/plan-after-committing-the-filter.png", "Execution tree after committing the filter, with the Matrix and Cases row reporting Plan 8 and the group and suite totals reduced by sixteen", "large") }}
<p>The test's plan drops to <strong>8</strong>, and the totals above it follow: the
	<strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong> group falls from <code>153</code> to <code>137</code> and the
	<strong>Tutorial</strong> suite from <code>282</code> to <code>266</code> — sixteen fewer planned cases in each.
	A run started now executes the eight surviving cases and counts against this plan.</p>

<h2 id="filtering-several-dimensions">Filtering several dimensions</h2>
<p>Each dimension has its own filter, and they combine the way the matrix does. Keeping one algorithm, one seed and
	both heights leaves <code>1 × 1 × 2 × 2 = 4</code> cases: the surviving cases are the product of the values kept
	on every dimension, not a list of rows chosen by hand.</p>
<p>This is what makes filtering worth reaching for on a large matrix. Pinning three dimensions and leaving one open
	isolates that dimension's effect, and the case count states plainly how much work the next run is.</p>

<h2 id="undoing-a-filter">Undoing a filter</h2>
<p>A filter that could not be lifted would narrow the matrix for good. Click <strong>Reset To Defaults</strong> and
	confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/cases-after-reset-to-defaults.png", "Matrix and Cases run panel after Reset To Defaults, with all 24 cases restored and the Test Cases group header reading 24 of 24", "large") }}
<p>All three algorithms are back and the matrix produces its full <strong>24</strong> cases again. Values added
	with <strong>Add value</strong> are dropped by the same reset, since they were never part of the test's
	declared matrix.</p>
<p>Note what else the reset does: every field returns to its declared default, and a required field that has no
	default is cleared. <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> is one of those, so it has to be set again before
	the test can run. The confirmation dialog says so before the reset is applied.</p>
<p>To restore a single dimension without touching the rest of the configuration, use
	<strong>Reset to Defaults</strong> inside that dimension's own filter dialog instead.</p>

<h2 id="filtering-and-selection">Filtering, selection and the plan</h2>
<p>Three mechanisms narrow a run, and they sit at different levels:</p>
<p><strong>The matrix</strong>, declared in the test source, defines which dimensions exist and which values they
	can take. <strong>Dimension filters</strong> change the values a dimension offers for this configuration, so
	they change which cases are generated. <strong>Case selection</strong> leaves the generated cases in place and
	chooses which of them run. Only <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> commits any of it, and until
	then the execution tree keeps reporting the plan that is already there.</p>
<p>A dimension can also be told to draw its values at random instead of listing them, which replaces the value list
	rather than filtering it — while a random mode is active the list, <strong>Select All</strong> and
	<strong>Clear All</strong> are disabled, and <strong>Add value</strong> is hidden. That is the subject of
	<strong>Matrix Dimensions Randomization</strong>.</p>
